🧠 Core Design Principle: “Pluggable Systems”

You define interfaces (contracts) for each part of the system:

Engine model
Controller
Target/Reference model
Sensor model

Then each one can have multiple implementations.

🧩 1. Engine Model Interface (Swappable Physics)
Contract (what every engine must provide)

Every engine model must implement:

update state
accept inputs (throttle, fuel injection)
return outputs (air, fuel, rpm, air-fuel ratio)
Example implementations
🟢 Simple engine (your current version)
linear air model
simple torque equation
basic RPM update
🔵 Medium engine
manifold delay (air lag)
nonlinear fuel burn
smoother torque curve
🔴 Advanced engine (future extension)
volumetric efficiency model
turbo lag
temperature effects
YAML selection
engine:
  type: simple

or

engine:
  type: medium
Python structure idea
class EngineBase:
    def step(self, throttle, fuel_injection, dt):
        raise NotImplementedError
class SimpleEngine(EngineBase):
    ...
class MediumEngine(EngineBase):
    ...

Factory:

def create_engine(config):
    if config["engine"]["type"] == "simple":
        return SimpleEngine(config)
    elif config["engine"]["type"] == "medium":
        return MediumEngine(config)
🧠 2. Controller (C++ swap system)

Same idea — but in C++.

Interface:
class Controller {
public:
    virtual double compute(double error, double dt) = 0;
};
Implementations:
🟢 PID Controller
controller:
  type: pid
🔵 LQR Controller (future)
controller:
  type: lqr
🔴 Rule-based ECU logic
controller:
  type: lookup_table
Why this is strong:

You can compare:

PID vs LQR performance
stability vs responsiveness
robustness to noise
🧠 3. Target / Reference Model (VERY important for realism)

Instead of hardcoding target AFR:

Interface:
inputs: throttle, rpm
output: target air-fuel ratio
Implementations:
🟢 Simple lookup
fixed AFR based on throttle
🔵 Engine-map style
2D table (throttle × rpm)
🔴 Dynamic model
efficiency + emissions + power trade-off
YAML:
reference_model:
  type: lookup
🧠 4. Sensor Model (noise abstraction)
Interface:
true state → measured state
Implementations:
🟢 No noise
perfect measurement
🔵 Gaussian noise
realistic sensor noise
🔴 Delayed + noisy sensor
adds lag + filtering effects
YAML:
sensor:
  type: noisy
  noise_std: 0.02
🔁 5. Full System Wiring (important concept)

At runtime:

YAML config
   ↓
Factory builds components
   ↓
Simulation loop runs:
   Engine ← Controller ← Sensor ← Reference
🧩 6. Main Simulation Loop (clean architecture)
engine = create_engine(config)
controller = create_controller(config)
reference = create_reference(config)
sensor = create_sensor(config)

while running:
    throttle = get_throttle(t)

    state = engine.get_state()

    target = reference.compute(throttle, state.rpm)

    measured = sensor.read(state)

    error = target - measured

    control = controller.compute(error, dt)

    engine.step(throttle, control, dt)
🧠 7. Why YAML switching is powerful

You can now run experiments like:

Experiment 1
engine: simple
controller: pid
Experiment 2
engine: medium
controller: pid
Experiment 3
engine: medium
controller: lqr

Now you can compare:

stability
overshoot
response time
robustness
🔥 8. What this achieves academically

This architecture demonstrates:

modular system design
abstraction boundaries
experimental reproducibility
controller comparison framework

This is far more valuable than the simulation itself.

